ch  panel for travel or whatever  circumstance they might want to order  but that's pretty much a clinician  decision and not based in the laboratory  dr. Morgan how would you recommend a  small lab that sends out their own PS to  incorporate the parasitic panel with  send out of Ont um I think that I  understand this question in that it  would prevent you from having to do a  send out of course the panel covers the  three most common parasites you might  still have to send out a few things if  the clinicians want to investigate other  parasitic possibilities but I think it's  a wonderful option for bringing in your  parasitology screening into your  laboratory and preventing send out tests  I think that would be a wonderful  application of that enteric parasite  panel I would like to take our audience  for so many great questions we have so  many coming in we have time for just a  couple more but I do want to remind our  audience that any questions we did not  have time for today and those that are  submitted during the on-demand period  will be addressed by dr. Morgan via the  contact information you provided at the  time of registration so dr. Morgan let's  sit and just a couple more what is the  physician insists on running c-diff on  hard stool samples oh what a painful in  question and you know this early on when  we've implemented doing molecular  testing which is now about three years  ago or so I've lost track it just seems  like a blur we had more issues with the  rejection of stools and more calls from  physicians I remember one famous one  early on when I actually sent a video of  the stool as I shook it in the container  to show the clinician how hard the stool  really was it sometimes clinicians don't  see the patient's stool the patient  tells them they have diarrhea or the  nurse might say they have diarrhea but  by the time they collect a stool the  stool solid again so it's not just  sometimes as I said the clinician is  unaware that the stool is corrected to  solid so if the techs get into  issues with physicians and have trouble  expressing their opinion about the stool  I'll take it over and talk to them and  assure them that we were correct in that  the stool is actually hard and that this  is not appropriate testing per our  testing algorithm that's what we do  Thank You dr. Morgan and we'll wrap with  this final question how are you  maintaining expertise for your cat  parasitic challenges with your  diminished microscopic time in training  that's an excellent question and near  and dear to my heart since our cap  inspection is next week  and we're very concerned with training  and competency of course all the time we  never saw a lot of parasites to begin  with I'm based in you know the middle of  Los Angeles with a very immune  suppressed non-traveling population  really we don't have a traveling clinic  or anywhere where we see unusual  parasites so we didn't get a lot of  experience looking at parasites from  doing a patient specimens we do still  maintain our parasitology lab and still  have ongoing processing so the texts do  still have ongoing competency and daily  at least twice a week  concentration of a few samples so they  do not lose that ability we of course do  competency every year we have cap  specimens coming to our laboratory but  we that's the way we keep it up but our  prevalence of parasites was so terribly  low I do not believe that they received  a lot of long going competency from our  patient specimens so we try to do it for  pictures cap specimens and things like  that to keep up their competency I think  that might end up yeah sorry about that  yes I does um conclude our Q&amp;A dr.  Morgan thank you for your time today and  your important involvement in optimizing  GI testing do you have any final  comments for our audience no I just  think that um I guess my final comment  would be  we have really found a great ability to  become more efficient and we think  provide very rapid and good results to  our clinicians using molecular testing  it always has to be done in the best of  circumstances with the best of sterility  techniques and I think if you really  work to do a good package you're really  supplying very good information for the  patient and at the same time making your  laboratory far more efficient and with  that I'll close  thank you again dr. Morgan we'd also  like to thank fabrics and our sponsor BV  for underwriting today's educational  webcast one more time I'd like to thank  the audience for joining us today and  for their interesting questions again we  did have a lot of questions that we did  not have time to answer and dr. Morgan  will answer those following the  presentation via email  now this webcast can be viewed on-demand  through August 2019 lab routes will  alert you via email when it's available  for replay we encourage you to share  that email with your colleagues who may  have missed today's live event a quick  poll will now pop up on your screen your  answers and suggestions will help  improve our webinar program and are  greatly appreciated thanks for joining  us until next time goodbye
 

